====================================================================================================
(1) OBSTRUCTION 1: the Bernoulli part of a single summand a*Bernoulli(p)
====================================================================================================
      a      p     vartheta   (their Theorem 1.7 needs this > 0)
      3   0.50     0.000000
      3   0.30     0.000000
      5   0.50     0.000000
      5   0.30     0.000000
      7   0.50     0.000000
      7   0.30     0.000000
    101   0.50     0.000000
    101   0.30     0.000000
  Exactly zero, for every odd a >= 3 and every p: the law charges 0 and a only, and
  those are never adjacent.  Theta_n = 0 and Corollary 1.8 is vacuous.  Their own
  Remark 1.3 names this degenerate case.

====================================================================================================
(2) THE REPAIR: block the sequence.  How large must a block be before vartheta > 0?
====================================================================================================
  sequence                      block   m     p     vartheta
      odds                        [1]   1  0.50     0.500000
      odds                     [1, 3]   2  0.50     0.500000
      odds                  [1, 3, 5]   3  0.50     0.625000
      odds               [1, 3, 5, 7]   4  0.50     0.750000
      odds            [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]   5  0.50     0.812500

    primes                        [3]   1  0.50     0.000000
    primes                     [3, 5]   2  0.50     0.000000
    primes                  [3, 5, 7]   3  0.50     0.125000
    primes              [3, 5, 7, 11]   4  0.50     0.375000
    primes          [3, 5, 7, 11, 13]   5  0.50     0.531250

====================================================================================================
(3a) A CORRECTION TO MY OWN FIRST PASS.  Blocking by three gave a Theta that did not
     grow with k at all -- 0.6250 for the odd numbers at every k -- and the reason is
     that only the FIRST block contributes.  A block of three LARGE elements, say
     {7,9,11}, has subset sums {0,7,9,11,16,18,20,27}: no two adjacent, vartheta = 0.
     Adjacency needs the block to have more subset sums than its span, so the block
     size must GROW.  vartheta by block position, m = 3:
====================================================================================================
  sequence                  block   vartheta
      odds              [1, 3, 5]   0.625000
      odds             [7, 9, 11]   0.000000
      odds           [19, 21, 23]   0.000000
      odds           [43, 45, 47]   0.000000

    primes              [3, 5, 7]   0.125000
    primes           [11, 13, 17]   0.000000
    primes           [31, 37, 41]   0.000000
    primes           [83, 89, 97]   0.000000

====================================================================================================
(3b) So: how large must the LAST block be before vartheta > 0?
     Heuristic before measuring: a block of m elements has 2^m subset sums spread over
     a span of about m * a_max, so adjacency needs 2^m >~ m * a_max, i.e. m >~ log2 k.
====================================================================================================
  sequence     k   a_max   min m with last-block vartheta>0  log2(k)
      odds    16      31                                  7     4.00
      odds    32      63                                 11     5.00
      odds    64     127                                 15     6.00
      odds   128     255                               None     7.00

    primes    16      59                                  7     4.00
    primes    32     137                                 10     5.00
    primes    64     313                                 15     6.00
    primes   128     727                               None     7.00

====================================================================================================
(3c) OBSTRUCTION 2, with blocks of the size (3b) forces.
     Rem 1.9 quantity  R := (Var/Theta)^(1/2) * (H + 1/Theta),  H ~ Lyapunov ratio.
     Their condition is R -> 0.  The test is the TREND in k.
====================================================================================================
  sequence     k    m  #blocks     Theta           Var         H            R
      odds    16    7        2    1.0000        1364.0   0.32460        48.92
      odds    32   11        2    1.2002       10920.0   0.22961       101.38  UP
      odds    64   15        4    1.3832       87376.0   0.16237       222.51  UP
      odds   128   20        6    1.5853      699040.0   0.11482       495.14  UP

    primes    16    7        2    0.9375        4188.0   0.34097        94.08
    primes    32   10        3    1.2480       43690.0   0.24160       195.12  UP
    primes    64   15        4    1.5746      471840.0   0.17056       441.01  UP
    primes   128   20        6    1.9223     5012920.0   0.12025      1034.24  UP

  R grows with k on both sequences, so Giuliano-Weber Remark 1.9 is violated, not
  merely unverified.  The reason is structural: their Var grows like the NUMBER of
  summands, ours like its cube, because our summands carry weights a_j ~ j.  Blocking
  makes it worse, not better: it buys vartheta > 0 at the cost of Theta <~ k/log k.

  VERDICT for paper 3 rem:closing.  The Bernoulli-part-extraction route cannot be
  quoted for prop:tiltlclt.  Obstruction 1 is repairable by blocking; obstruction 2 is
  not, and it is the same feature -- large weights -- that makes our LCLT interesting.
  The Edgeworth constants have to be derived, not imported.
